course: medicine
lesson: 14
title: Asthma and inhalers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In asthma the airways in the lungs are extra sensitive: colds, exercise, cold air, dust or smoke make them tighten and swell, causing wheeze, cough and breathlessness.
explain: The blue reliever inhaler relaxes the airway muscles within minutes, like loosening a tight belt round the airways.
explain: The brown preventer inhaler calms the swelling slowly, so it only works if taken every day, even when you feel fine.
explain: A spacer, a plastic tube on the inhaler, helps more medicine reach the lungs instead of sticking in the mouth.
explain: Asthma is not catching and not a sign of being unfit: with good control people with asthma can do every sport, and many top athletes have it.
fact: In asthma the airways are sensitive, and triggers such as colds, exercise, cold air, dust or smoke make them tighten and swell.
fact: The blue reliever inhaler relaxes the airway muscles within minutes; inhalers control asthma but do not cure it.
fact: A preventer inhaler contains a steroid that calms airway swelling over time and only works if taken every day.
fact: Inhaled steroids are not the same as the steroids some athletes misuse.
fact: A spacer holds the puff so it can be breathed in slowly, so more medicine reaches the lungs.
fact: In an asthma attack the person sits upright, uses the blue inhaler with a spacer, up to 10 puffs, and 999 is called if they are not better or get worse.
fact: With good control people with asthma can do all sports, and many top athletes have asthma; warming up, and using the reliever before exercise if needed, helps.
fact: Smoke irritates the airways; homes and cars should be smoke-free, and smoking out of the window is not enough.
fact: UK schools can keep emergency reliever inhalers for children known to have asthma.
fact: A peak flow meter measures how fast you can blow air out, which shows how open the airways are.
fact: Coughing at night can be a sign that asthma is not well controlled.
fact: Colds and other viral infections are the most common trigger of asthma attacks in children.
exercise: What happens to the airways in asthma? | They tighten and swell when triggered | They slowly fill up with water from a cold | They grow too big for the chest | They stop working for ever after an attack
exercise: What does the blue reliever inhaler do? | Relaxes the airway muscles within minutes | Cures asthma for ever after a few puffs | Calms swelling slowly over weeks | Makes you sleepy so your breathing slows down
exercise: Why take the brown preventer every day even when you feel fine? | It only works if taken every day | It tastes nice and sweet in the morning | It is a vitamin for the lungs | It cures colds before they start
exercise: Are inhaled steroids the same as steroids athletes misuse? | No, they are different | Yes, exactly the same stuff | Yes, they make big muscles | No, they are just sweets
exercise: What is a spacer for? | Getting more medicine into the lungs | Keeping the inhaler warm in winter | Making the puff louder so you hear it | Storing spare medicine for later
exercise: What should a child having an asthma attack do? | Sit upright and use the blue inhaler | Lie down flat on the floor and keep still | Run it off round the field | Hold his breath for as long as he can
exercise: How many puffs of the blue inhaler can be given with a spacer in an attack? | Up to 10 | Only 1, never more | 50 or more | None at all
exercise: Can children with asthma do sport? | Yes, with good control they can do all sports | No, never, not even a little | Only chess and other quiet games | Only swimming
exercise: Why is smoking near a child with asthma bad? | Smoke irritates the airways | Smoke only smells bad on clothes | Smoke makes you hungry | It isn't, smoke outside a window is fine
exercise: Who can use the school's emergency inhaler? | Children already known to have asthma | Anyone at all who is out of breath after running | Only the teachers | Children with colds
exercise: What does a peak flow meter measure? | How fast you can blow air out | Your temperature through the mouth | How tall you are for your age | How loud you can cough
exercise: What can waking up coughing at night mean for a child with asthma? | The asthma may not be well controlled | Nothing, it's normal for everyone | The pillow is too soft and feathery | He has caught a cold for sure
exercise: What is the most common trigger of asthma attacks in children? | Colds and other virus infections | Eating carrots and other vegetables | Reading books in bed | Drinking cold water too quickly
exercise: Is asthma catching? | No | Yes, by coughing near someone | Yes, by sharing inhalers | Only in winter
exercise: What should happen if the blue inhaler isn't helping in an attack? | Call 999 | Wait until tomorrow morning | Go for a short run | Lie down flat and try to sleep
test: mate's wheezing in PE, someone says lie him down. yeah? | No, sit him upright and get his blue inhaler | Yeah, lying flat on the mat helps him breathe | Yeah, and give him a drink | No, make him run another lap to open it up
test: Sam says people with asthma are just unfit. What's true? | Asthma is about sensitive airways; many top athletes have it | He's right, they just need to train harder | Asthma means you're lazy | Only unfit people ever get it
test: Dad smokes out of the kitchen window so my asthma won't mind. Safe? | No, smoke still gets in; homes should be smoke-free | Yes, an open window stops all the smoke getting in | Yes, if it's open wide | No, only cigars count
test: I feel fine so I'll skip my brown inhaler this week. What happens? | The preventer won't work properly | Nothing, it's only meant for the bad days | It works better if you skip a week | It turns into a blue one
test: In a cartoon, a boy takes one puff and is cured of asthma for life. What's wrong? | Inhalers control asthma, they don't cure it | Nothing, one puff cures it for ever | Asthma isn't real | Inhalers only make asthma worse over time
test: What would happen if Leo didn't use a spacer? | More medicine might stick in his mouth instead of reaching his lungs | Nothing would reach his mouth | The inhaler would explode | He'd catch a cold from the inhaler
test: My cold's started and my asthma's getting worse. Is that weird? | No, colds are the commonest trigger | Yes, colds usually help asthma | Yes, it means a new illness is starting | No, it means you're nearly cured
test: Ollie forgot his inhaler on the trip and has an attack. School has an emergency one. Can he use it? | Yes, if he's known to have asthma | No, never, it belongs to the school | Only after a week | Only if he pays for it
test: Why does the nurse make you blow into a tube at your check? | To see how open your airways are | To test how well you can sing and whistle | To clean the dust out of your lungs | To measure how tall you've grown
test: Grandma says don't do PE with asthma. What do doctors say? | Exercise is good; use your reliever before if needed and warm up | Never exercise at all, it can bring on an attack | Only exercise on Sundays | Exercise every day cures asthma completely in the end
exam: Identify the action of a reliever inhaler. | It relaxes the muscles of the airways within minutes | It reduces airway inflammation gradually over several weeks | It eliminates asthma permanently | It induces sleep and thereby slows the breathing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of a preventer inhaler? | It must be taken daily, irrespective of symptoms | It is to be taken only during an attack | It is identical to the anabolic steroids misused by athletes | It is to be discontinued when the child feels well
exam: It is given that a child is experiencing an asthma attack. Select the appropriate posture. | Seated upright | Lying flat upon the back | Standing, with the arms raised | Lying upon the front
exam: State the most common precipitant of asthma attacks in children. | Viral infections such as the common cold | Consumption of vegetables at mealtimes | Prolonged reading | Drinking cold water rapidly
